# Obsidian Plugin Development Standards

## Context
- When developing Obsidian plugins
- When using CodeMirror editor API
- When handling Markdown rendering and editing functionality
- When implementing user interface elements

## Requirements

### Obsidian API Standards
- Use TypeScript type definitions provided by Obsidian
- Follow plugin lifecycle methods (onload/onunload)
- Use Obsidian's settings API to store user configurations
- Create consistent user interfaces with Obsidian's UI components
- Avoid direct DOM manipulation, prioritize Obsidian API

### CodeMirror Integration Standards
- Use CodeMirror 6 API for editor extensions
- Use EditorView and EditorState for state management
- Follow Extension interface when implementing custom extensions
- Handle editor state changes with Transactions
- Avoid frequent redraws that cause performance issues

### Performance Considerations
- Avoid executing time-consuming operations on the main thread
- Use debouncing and throttling for frequent events
- Implement lazy loading and render on demand
- Avoid excessive use of MutationObserver
- Optimize memory usage for large document processing

### Project Structure
- Use standard Obsidian plugin template structure
- Separate business logic from UI components
- Use modular design for maintainability
- Follow manifest.json specifications for plugin metadata
- Use version control to manage dependencies

## Examples

<example>
// ✅ Correct plugin initialization
export default class MyPlugin extends Plugin {
  async onload() {
    // Register commands
    this.addCommand({
      id: 'my-command',
      name: 'Execute My Command',
      callback: () => this.myFeature()
    });
    
    // Register settings
    this.addSettingTab(new MyPluginSettingTab(this.app, this));
    
    // Register editor extensions
    this.registerEditorExtension([
      myCustomExtension()
    ]);
  }
  
  onunload() {
    // Clean up resources
  }
}
</example>

<example type="invalid">
// ❌ Incorrect CodeMirror usage
export default class BadPlugin extends Plugin {
  onload() {
    // Direct DOM manipulation instead of using API
    document.querySelector('.CodeMirror').addEventListener('click', () => {
      // Directly modifying editor content
      editor.setValue('New content');
    });
    
    // Not using Obsidian's settings API
    localStorage.setItem('my-plugin-settings', JSON.stringify(settings));
  }
}
</example>

## Critical Rules
  - Always use Obsidian's provided APIs rather than direct DOM manipulation
  - Correctly implement plugin onload and onunload lifecycle methods
  - Use CodeMirror 6's state management model instead of directly modifying editor content
  - Store all user settings through Obsidian's settings API
  - Follow Obsidian's design language to ensure UI consistency
  - Use asynchronous operations for tasks that might block the main thread
  - Clean up all resources and event listeners when the plugin is unloaded 